---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const life = assertion('lima-tracts-live-five-years-less')
const gap = assertion('the-health-gap-is-a-gap-in-conditions')

const lifeSpec = bar(
  life.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  life.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Years of life expectancy at birth', labelValues: true },
)

const spreadSpec = bar(
  gap.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  gap.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of adults, as estimated', tickSuffix: '%' },
)
---

<Base
  title="Health"
  lede="How long people live in Allen County, how far that varies inside it, and what a model can and cannot be asked."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Thirteen years, inside one county</h2>
    <p>
      For twelve phases this corpus counted the people of Allen County and held nothing about how
      long any of them lived. The National Center for Health Statistics built a life table for
      almost every census tract in the United States from death certificates and population, and
      Allen County has thirty of them.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={life} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="life"
      title="Life expectancy at birth by census tract, 2010–2015"
      caption="NCHS USALEEP. The two ends are more than five combined standard errors apart, on published errors of about 1.7 years each. 'Lima tracts' are the thirteen whose internal point falls inside the city, which is a statement about where a tract's centre is and not about where its people live."
      columns={['', 'Years']}
      rows={life.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="life" set:html={JSON.stringify(lifeSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The two groups barely overlap. The best Lima tract expects 78.2 years and the worst tract
      outside the city expects 74.0, so eleven of the thirteen inside sit below every tract outside
      but one. Weighted by population it is 73.9 years against 79.1.
    </p>
    <p>
      No county figure is given here, and the reason is worth stating. Two of the file's tracts were
      split when the census redrew its geography in 2020 and seven of the county's current tracts
      have no 2010 predecessor with a published estimate, so the thirty rows cover 82,484 of the
      county's 102,206 people. Four fifths of a county is not a county. Against Ohio's 2,772 tracts,
      which run from 60.0 to 89.2 years, Allen County's median sits half a year above the state's.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the difference is made of, and what it is not</h2>
    <p>
      The county's other health source covers forty measures at three geographies, and it is a
      model rather than a measurement — CDC fits national survey responses to local demographic
      profiles, so nobody in these tracts was asked anything.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={gap} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="spread"
      title="Lowest and highest tract estimate, four measures"
      caption="CDC PLACES, crude prevalence, all thirty-five of the county's 2020 tracts. One tract holds the county's highest estimate on nine of ten measures examined; what is in it, this corpus cannot say."
      columns={['', 'Per cent']}
      rows={gap.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="spread" set:html={JSON.stringify(spreadSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Two measures corroborate each other, which is rare in a file like this. A quarter of Lima
      residents over sixty-five are estimated to have lost all their teeth, against a sixth
      countywide; and half the city's adults saw a dentist in the year against 58 per cent of the
      county's. Both gaps clear their intervals and they point the same way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The question this page will not answer</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious reading is that Lima's health gap is its poverty gap. This site holds the poverty
      gap — the city has a third of the county's people and 64.1 per cent of those below the
      poverty line, on a median household income 48 per cent of the township across its southern
      line — and it will not join the two.
    </p>
    <p>
      CDC names its own inputs: the Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System, Census population
      counts, <em>and the American Community Survey</em>. The survey that supplies this site's
      income and poverty figures is a covariate in the model that produces these health estimates.
      Reading the poverty back out of the health number is reading the model's input out of its
      output, and it would be worthless however true the conclusion happens to be.
    </p>
    <p>
      The life expectancy figures are not subject to that. USALEEP counts deaths — actual
      certificates, in the tract where they happened — and models only the smoothing. That is why
      this page states a life expectancy for a Lima tract and declines to state a diabetes
      prevalence for one. The difference is not confidence; both files publish a standard error. It
      is what was counted.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
